# Quicksort Implementation in C with Detailed Comments

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

// Function to swap two elements in an array
void swap(int* arr, int i, int j) {
    int temp = arr[i];
    arr[i] = arr[j];
    arr[j] = temp;
}

// Partition function: divides array into two parts around a pivot
// Returns the final position of the pivot element
int partition(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    // Choose the last element as pivot
    int pivot = arr[high];
    int i = low - 1;  // Index of smaller element

    // Iterate through array and place elements smaller than pivot
    for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
        if (arr[j] <= pivot) {
            i++;
            swap(arr, i, j);
        }
    }

    // Place pivot in correct position
    swap(arr, i + 1, high);
    return i + 1;
}

// Quicksort function: recursive implementation
void quicksort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    // Base case: if array has 1 or 0 elements, it's already sorted
    if (low < high) {
        // Partition the array and get pivot index
        int pi = partition(arr, low, high);

        // Recursively sort elements before and after partition
        quicksort(arr, low, pi - 1);
        quicksort(arr, pi + 1, high);
    }
}

// Function to print array elements
void printArray(int arr[], int size) {
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");
}

// Function to generate random array for testing
void generateRandomArray(int arr[], int size) {
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        arr[i] = rand() % 100;  // Random number between 0 and 99
    }
}

// Main function with test cases
int main() {
    // Test Case 1: Simple array
    int arr1[] = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
    int n1 = sizeof(arr1) / sizeof(arr1[0]);
    printf("Original array: ");
    printArray(arr1, n1);
    quicksort(arr1, 0, n1 - 1);
    printf("Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr1, n1);

    // Test Case 2: Array with duplicates
    int arr2[] = {5, 2, 8, 2, 9, 1, 5, 5};
    int n2 = sizeof(arr2) / sizeof(arr2[0]);
    printf("\nOriginal array: ");
    printArray(arr2, n2);
    quicksort(arr2, 0, n2 - 1);
    printf("Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr2, n2);

    // Test Case 3: Already sorted array
    int arr3[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7};
    int n3 = sizeof(arr3) / sizeof(arr3[0]);
    printf("\nOriginal array: ");
    printArray(arr3, n3);
    quicksort(arr3, 0, n3 - 1);
    printf("Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr3, n3);

    // Test Case 4: Reverse sorted array
    int arr4[] = {7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1};
    int n4 = sizeof(arr4) / sizeof(arr4[0]);
    printf("\nOriginal array: ");
    printArray(arr4, n4);
    quicksort(arr4, 0, n4 - 1);
    printf("Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr4, n4);

    // Test Case 5: Random array
    int arr5[10];
    generateRandomArray(arr5, 10);
    printf("\nRandom array:   ");
    printArray(arr5, 10);
    quicksort(arr5, 0, 9);
    printf("Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr5, 10);

    return 0;
}
```

## Key Features Explained

### 1. **Partition Function**
- Selects the last element as the pivot
- Moves all elements smaller than pivot to the left
- Places pivot in its correct sorted position
- Returns the pivot's final index

### 2. **Quicksort Function**
- Uses divide-and-conquer approach
- Recursively sorts left and right subarrays
- Base case: when low >= high, array is sorted

### 3. **Time Complexity**
- **Best/Average Case:** O(n log n)
- **Worst Case:** O(n²) (when array is already sorted)
- **Space Complexity:** O(log n) for recursion stack

### 4. **Test Cases**
- Simple unsorted array
- Array with duplicates
- Already sorted array
- Reverse sorted array
- Random array

## Compilation and Execution

```bash
gcc -o quicksort quicksort.c
./quicksort
```

